    "lawCode": "Amd §99, Work Comp L",
    "lawSection": "Workers' Compensation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5540\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the workers' compensation law, in\nrelation to requiring the state\ninsurance fund to file annual audited financial statements with the\noffice of the state comptroller\n\nPURPOSE:\nRequires the State Insurance Fund to file annual audited\nfinancial statements with the Office of the State Comptroller.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 amends Section 99 of the Workers'\nCompensation Law to require the State Insurance Fund to annually file\nwith the Office of the State Comptroller, on or before June\nfifteenth, financial statements audited by an independent auditor.\n\nSection 2 provides for an effective date of the first of January next\nsucceeding the date on which it shall have become a law.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe Bureau of Financial Reporting has concluded that\nthe Government Accounting Standards Board considers the State\nInsurance Fund (SIF) a component unit of the State of New York\nreporting entity and requires its audited financial information to be\ndiscretely presented in the New York State Comprehensive Annual\nFinancial Report (CAFR). This entity is not required by statute to\nfile audited financial reports with the Office of the State\nComptroller (OSC). This bill proposes to amend existing state law to\nrequire that this entity file annual audited financial statements\nwith OSC.\n\nPursuant to Section 8 of the State Finance Law, OSC must complete its\nannual financial report and distribute it within 120 days of the\nstate's March 31 fiscal year end. In 2007 and 2008, the SIF was late\nin making financial reports available to OSC for inclusion in the\nCAFR. Requiring the SIF to meet a statutory delivery date will ensure\nthe timely completion and high quality of the CAFR.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2009:  S.4463 Passed Senate; A.8399 Referred to Insurance\n2010:  S.4463 Passed Senate; A.8399 Referred to Insurance\n\nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\nThis bill has no significant fiscal impact.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis bill takes effect on the first of January next\nsucceeding the date on which it shall have become a law.\n\nThe Comptroller urges passage of this legislation.\n\n\n",
